# Development notes

Reviewed against the implementation and current project direction on 2026-10-29.
These are candidates for investigation, not a committed roadmap or release
requirements. See README.md for supported features and development status.

TINQ's current focus is expression and text-template parsing, compilation,
evaluation, list filters, and optional explicit lowered-frame access. Keep the
library broadly type-agnostic and usable independently. Host applications own
their static type/model resolution and application-specific evaluation policies.

## Remaining maintenance and investigation

- Finish reviewing inherited Angular-style watch/input machinery.
  Watch discovery and input-function generation are disabled, but compiler
  watchId handling and empty input/assignment scaffolding remain in
  src/astcompiler.mjs. Preserve active constant analysis. Keep host applications' dependency tracking separate, and
  do not remove the legacy assign/inputs argument slots without reviewing
  compatibility with the current scope, env, assign, inputs signature.

- Assess whether broader normalized-expression cache reuse is worthwhile.
  compileExpression already caches source strings, adds a normalized-string
  alias, and caches AST inputs using serialization plus lowered frame metadata.
  A previously unseen formatting variant can still compile a separate function.
  Any additional canonicalization must preserve lowering semantics and the
  expected source-location metadata. Establish a useful workload before adding
  cache complexity.

- Investigate source maps or other debugging aids for generated JavaScript.
  Interpreter mode already exists; mapping generated code back to an expression
  or template is a separate, still-open debugging idea. AST source locations
  alone are not generated-code source maps.

## Ideas requiring a separate design decision

- Additional expression syntax: template literals, spread, exponentiation, or
  named iteration variables. Evaluate each feature against concrete template
  use cases and compatibility, rather than treating JavaScript feature parity
  as a goal. Existing list filters and list fields already handle iteration;
  a general for...of statement would extend the language beyond that model.

- True expression-local variables or explicit expression parameters.
  Assignment expressions already work, but declarations and lexical parameter
  scopes would need their own design. Preserve current assignment behavior
  while assessing this; the env argument is for lowered frames, and assign is
  the legacy assignment-helper slot, not a new locals/parameters object.

- Promise-aware expression or template evaluation.
  Core evaluation is synchronous. External composition and batching can describe
  work that a consuming application performs asynchronously; this is not the
  same as awaiting promises inside expressions or text templates. Any native
  async API needs an explicit use case and compatibility plan.

## Host application integration

Keep application-specific integration planning with the host applications.
Propose any needed generic TINQ extensions separately.

- Preserve the plain-data/value API. Host applications can adapt inputs,
  outputs, and error handling to their own runtime conventions.

- Host applications can resolve and lower expressions using their own models.
  TINQ supports optional explicit env frames, including named list/lambda
  frames, while retaining ordinary dynamic lookup for unlowered expressions.
  Preserve current this/$locals compatibility; $locals serializes as this.

- External-composition interfaces can support host applications' file assembly
  workflows. File-format implementation and deployment infrastructure remain
  outside the core library.
